# What `composer require` actually downloads.
#
# Without this file every tracked path lands in the dist archive, so each
# consumer pulled the test suite, the CI workflow, the Docker dev environment
# and the proto-sync tooling into their vendor/ directory. None of it is usable
# by someone installing the client, and some of it describes how the service is
# built rather than how to call it — the CI workflow names the engine's private
# repository, and the sync scripts explain the relationship between the two.
#
# This bounds the archive, not the repository: the repository has to stay public
# for Packagist to install from it, so anyone who looks can still read all of
# this on GitHub. What it stops is shipping it to every consumer by default.

# The generated PHP classes carry the descriptor inside them, so nothing in the
# shipped archive reads this file — the `# source: engine.proto` line at the top
# of each generated class is a comment. Keeping it out means a consumer's vendor
# directory does not carry a readable copy of the service contract it never uses.
# It stays in the repository: regenerating the stubs needs it.
/proto              export-ignore

/tests              export-ignore
/scripts            export-ignore
/.github            export-ignore
/database           export-ignore
/docs               export-ignore
/Dockerfile         export-ignore
/Dockerfile.frankenphp export-ignore
/docker-compose.yml export-ignore
/.dockerignore      export-ignore
/phpunit.xml        export-ignore
/.gitattributes     export-ignore
/.gitignore         export-ignore
